WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:16.630
The bouquet of bell-shaped organisms you see in the middle of the screen right now are Vorticella.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:22.560
In their youth, these protists are swimmers,
moving around to gather food and grow.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:29.570
But as adults, Vorticella like to settle down,
using their long stalks to attach to bits

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.360
of rock, algae, or even animals.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.400
Of course, sometimes multiple Vorticella find
the same prime real estate.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.589
But when you’re small, it’s easy to share
space, and that is why you’ll find groups

00:00:43.589 --> 00:00:45.580
of vorticella like this one.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.720
It’s not technically a colony.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.760
Think of it more as a community of like-minded
individuals.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.519
And today, we are going to join them.

00:00:55.519 --> 00:01:00.910
They may not seem to have the most exciting
lifestyle, I hear that.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:06.520
But even in the microcosmos, constancy comes
with its own small thrills.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:13.440
So today, we’re going to stick with these
Vorticella for 12 minutes of unedited footage

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.040
and get to know them better while also meeting
some of their strange visitors.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:27.080
We’ve been visited several times now by
this delightfully blue blob that kind of resembles

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:32.060
a whale thanks to its relatively large size
and oceanic hue.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:38.060
Of course, it is not a whale, but it is one
of our favorite giants of the microcosmos:

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.350
Stentor coeruleus.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:42.810
Like vorticella, Stentors are ciliates.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:48.860
And while this stentor is swimming freely,
they are known to attach to surfaces as well.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:54.280
These attached stentors will take on the distinctive
trumpet shape that they are known and named

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:01.060
for, making them look like a larger, more
exaggerated form of the vorticella’s bell shape.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:15.080
One of the striking movements you’ll notice
happening again and again is that rapid contraction

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.250
of the vorticella whenever they are touched.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:24.200
This movement is likely a protective maneuver,
allowing the organisms to evade predators

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.730
and adapt to turbulent waters.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:33.139
This movement is created by a thin fiber inside
the stalk called the myoneme, which contracts

00:02:33.139 --> 00:02:35.519
rapidly into a coil.

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:41.239
This movement is very fast, as you probably
have been witnessing, but watch carefully

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:47.280
and you might be able to see some of those
tight coils as the vorticella contract and

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.000
relax again.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.379
In the upper left corner here, you can see
what looks like a cross between a spider and

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:12.879
the Millennium Falcon.

00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:16.019
Now again, it is of course neither of those
things.

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:17.629
This is a nauplius.

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:23.939
When Otto Friedrich Müller first observed
the nauplius in the 18th century, he thought

00:03:23.939 --> 00:03:30.650
it was its own species—just one of those
strange, adult organisms of the microcosmos.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:36.370
But eventually scientists understood that
this organism is actually the early larval

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.400
stage of some kind of crustacean.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:45.000
And in about a minute, we will see one of
the crustaceans it might grow up to become.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:39.820
This crustacean is a cyclops, one of the possible
creatures that the earlier nauplius might

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.250
eventually grow into.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:46.620
The inspiration for its name is obvious: what
else could you name an organism that only

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.670
has one large eye?

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:54.910
The cyclops of the microcosmos is, as we will
see, a bit obtrusive.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:05:00.620
But it is relatively harmless to us—that
is until and unless they become host to parasites

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:06.000
like the Guinea worm, which can do harm to
humans.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:53.280
Returning our attention to the vorticella,
while their adult forms don’t move around

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.960
much, they are still able to gather food ranging
from bacteria to smaller protists.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:05.340
They do this using the cilia around their
mouth, waving around the small hair-like structures

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.000
to create currents that will drive food into
their mouths.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:11.540
You may have noticed some smaller organisms
swimming and grazing amongst the vorticella.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:18.040
Many of these are rotifers, trying to find
the green algae you can also see zooming around.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:24.800
Or you can see them munching on the bacteria
and fungi that form the biofilm that the Vorticella

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.180
have attached themselves too.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:33.010
It is always so strange to realize that rotifers
are multicellular animals, while vorticella

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:35.550
are single-celled organisms.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:43.700
And yet rotifers are usually smaller than
their structurally simpler neighbors.

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:36.020
Like other microbes, vorticella are found
in aquatic habitats all over the world.

00:08:36.029 --> 00:08:44.510
But in 2012, a group of scientists found evidence
of a vorticella in a very strange location:

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:50.970
inside the 200 million year old fossil of
a leech cocoon from Antarctica.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:58.130
Yes, the vorticella-like organism was also
long dead, preserved in the polysaccharides

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:02.990
and fibrous proteins that made up the prehistoric
cocoon.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:07.900
Ciliates may be easy for us to find in our
own microscope samples, but these kinds of

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:14.730
fossilized remains are much more rare because
their soft bodies make for bad fossils.

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:21.240
The uniqueness of this kind of discovery is
matched by just how striking the similarities

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.540
are between the fossilized organism the scientists
found and the modern day Vorticella you can

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.120
see here.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:34.040
Even in the fossil, you can make out the distinct
bell-shaped body and helical stalk that so

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:39.980
closely resemble these Vorticella, living
right now before our eyes

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.120
I don’t know much about microbial etiquette,

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.980
but this cyclops
does seem kind of rude, like it’s just trying

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.480
to push its way through the vorticella.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:13.700
Then again, maybe the vorticella are the rude
ones, taking up all this space?

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:19.310
Or maybe we shouldn’t be pushing our human
sense of etiquette onto microbes.

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:24.700
Whatever the answer, the vorticella seem to
be holding their own quite well here.

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:30.340
While the vorticella contracted tightly in
response to the cyclops earlier, you can now

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:34.800
watch as they extend back to their normal
configuration.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:41.000
You might even see some of the tight coils
in their stalks in the moments before they relax.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:00.540
Vorticella were first described by the creator
of the microscope Antonie van Leeuwenhoek

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:04.620
himself in 1674.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:08.020
But you know who doesn’t care about that
distinction?

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:11.320
This cyclops, who is back for round two.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:59.600
For all the smaller organisms that dart in
and out, and for all the larger organisms

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:06.170
that rudely barrel through them, the vorticella
just rolls with the punches.

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:10.740
After 12 minutes of this, I’m starting to
feel like maybe I could learn a thing or two

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.080
from these little troopers.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:19.460
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:24.740
And thank you especially to all of these people
who support this show on Patreon.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:28.710
They are the reason this small team of people
can make this show.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:32.560
And we are really happy and grateful to be
able to do it.

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:37.140
If you’d like to join them, you can go to
patreon.com/journeytomicro

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:41.420
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James, you can check out Jam

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:43.040
&amp; Germs on Instagram.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:48.420
And if you want to see more from us, there’s
probably a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

